A||||5||Little Duce Coupe||Re-coloring interior parts||||10||
Z||000000||Little Duce Coupe||12-21-2001||01:09 PM||||<BR>Hey all,<P>I'm looking for some tips/addvice on re-coloring.<P>Not for my little coupe...srry I like the "beachwood" strange though I am.<P>I bought a Ford 250 that has a RED interior...arghhhh.<P>I would like to be simple with it and change the interior color to black.<P>1. Is this a professional only kind of thing?<BR>2. If not, is it something a shade tree mechanic might be able to do.<BR>and......if so where do you find the paint and what is the best way to apply?<P>Thanks in advance for any advice..I HATE RED.<P>But hey...the price was right.<P>------------------<BR>Little Duce Coupe<BR>"You Don't Know What She Costs"||||reg||10
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-21-2001||01:26 PM||||First off, dont feel alone...I LOVE  the 88 beechwood color myself. Second, changing the interior color is no big deal at all. Doesnt matter if its an F150 or a Fiero...its got vinyle interior parts. <BR>Most any automotive paint stores sells interior vinyle paints. If you have access to a spray gun setup, you can get the paint for that. If not, they have it in cans and it (believe it or not) works very well. Just remember the number one rule of thumb is having a CLEAN surface. If its got grease/dirt/armor all or anything like that, forget it. Denatured alcohol is the best cleaner. <BR>You can also find carpets anywhere..and Im sure somewhere has new fabric for the seats. Headliners are easy..use the search functio here to find out how to do that. If you've got any kind of automotive skills (for cosmetics), you shouldnt have a prob. Seat belts may be your only hangup.<BR>For a good reference, check out  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierolisa</A>   and go to where she did her interior color change. Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||12-21-2001||06:22 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.jcwhitney.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jcwhitney.com</A>  sells the spray to recolr your interior.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||12-22-2001||02:10 AM||||It's a huge pain in the arse, but it does look good when you finally get it done. You'll be kinda ticked when you're touching it up though!!!<P>I love my repainted interior (I got the paint from the auto paint store, just ordered from their paint chip book interior colors) and its a nice color, but there are places like door panels and center consoles that take extra abuse. I prepped mine with a layer of flexible vinyl spray and let that dry before using my interior paint (which I bought in a can and used a paint gun for) and that has helped a lot. But this winter, whenever I get back home, I will be doing touchups, even though I was pretty darn careful with it all summer.<P>The link My7 was referring to is <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/interior.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/interior.html</A>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Good Luck<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">                 <BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A> *<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa1/showdates.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Events 2002</A> *<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tarheel Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroLisa||12-22-2001||02:11 AM||||P.S. Prep the living he!! out of every part you paint with acetone and an old rag first!!!||||reg||1
